Acme's diagnosis is correct: `verify()` was falling back to `JSON.stringify(payload)` whenever it got a parsed object, so the HMAC was recomputed over re-serialized bytes instead of the bytes PayCo signed — that can never match. I've fixed the SDK so it no longer fails silently: 4.2.1 rejects any non-string/Buffer/Uint8Array payload with a `SignatureVerificationError` telling the caller to pass the raw body, and there's a regression test for it.

Their fix is the right one — capture the raw body before JSON parsing. With a global `express.json()`, use its `verify` callback:

```js
app.use(express.json({
  verify: (req, _res, buf) => { req.rawBody = buf; },
}));

app.post("/webhooks/payco", (req, res) => {
  const event = verify(req.rawBody, req.headers["payco-signature"], process.env.PAYCO_WEBHOOK_SECRET);
  res.sendStatus(200);
});
```

Or scope the parser to the route: `app.post("/webhooks/payco", express.raw({ type: "application/json" }), handler)` and verify `req.body` (a Buffer). Either way the signature must be checked against the exact bytes received, never `JSON.stringify(req.body)`. Upgrade to 4.2.1 so the mistake surfaces as a clear error rather than a confusing 400.
